INT. BELL FOUNDRY - CASTING FLOOR - NIGHT

Cold furnaces. Sand molds in rows like sleeping beasts. A crack in the skylight lets in one blade of moon.

DET. SABINE MOREAU-KIM, 40s, camel coat, kneels at an empty cradle where a bronze bell should hang. She runs a thumb along the chain mark.

ORLANDO PEEL, 60s, the foreman, stands with his arms crossed over a leather apron. THEODORA VASS, 30s, in a clean white shirt, watches him from the doorway. LIAM, 17, the apprentice, sits on an upturned bucket and doesn't look at anyone.

MOREAU-KIM
Four tons. Gone.

PEEL
I told you. I locked up at ten.

VASS
He always says ten.

LIAM
It was ten.

PEEL
It was ten.

MOREAU-KIM
Who has keys?

PEEL
Me.

VASS
Me.

LIAM
Me.

They answer nearly at once. The detective stands. Brushes sand from her knee.

PEEL
(to Liam)
You gave yours back.

LIAM
No.

VASS ^
(over him)
You gave it to me.

MOREAU-KIM
Which is it?

Silence. The kid studies his boots. Peel's jaw tightens. Vass crosses to the cradle, touches the chain, and for a second her hand shakes.

MOREAU-KIM (CONT'D)
Thank you, Ms. Vass. That's all I needed.
